1
00:00:03,550 --> 00:00:05,525
Ten million documents.

2
00:00:05,825 --> 00:00:10,682
A question comes in, and you have about two
and a half seconds to find the right

3
00:00:10,682 --> 00:00:13,900
passages and write a grounded
answer, with citations.

4
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:19,596
In this video, we'll design that system end to
end: how to size it, how to build it,

5
00:00:19,596 --> 00:00:21,592
and how to know whether it works.

6
00:00:22,617 --> 00:00:25,417
Here's the idea that shapes
everything else.

7
00:00:25,717 --> 00:00:31,117
Treat a RAG system as a retrieval system
first, and a generation system second.

8
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:34,342
Most wrong answers start in retrieval.

9
00:00:34,642 --> 00:00:39,117
The right passage was never found, or it
never made it into the prompt.

10
00:00:39,417 --> 00:00:42,967
And retrieval is also the
cheapest part to measure.

11
00:00:43,983 --> 00:00:49,141
Before choosing a single component, pin down a
few requirements, because each one

12
00:00:49,141 --> 00:00:50,733
changes the architecture.

13
00:00:51,350 --> 00:00:53,525
First, what are the documents?

14
00:00:53,825 --> 00:00:59,675
Ten million documents of about five pages
each is roughly 25B tokens.

15
00:00:59,975 --> 00:01:03,600
That's a very different system
from ten million tweets.

16
00:01:04,217 --> 00:01:06,567
Second, what kinds of questions?

17
00:01:06,867 --> 00:01:11,894
Simple lookups, multi-step questions, and
find-everything requests each need

18
00:01:11,894 --> 00:01:13,217
different machinery.

19
00:01:13,817 --> 00:01:16,042
Third, load and latency.

20
00:01:16,342 --> 00:01:23,092
A workable starting point is 50 queries per
second at peak, p95 under 3 s end to

21
00:01:23,092 --> 00:01:26,467
end, and the first token in under 1.5 s.

22
00:01:27,083 --> 00:01:28,883
Fourth, freshness.

23
00:01:29,183 --> 00:01:34,883
If 1% of the corpus changes daily,
that's 100k documents a day.

24
00:01:35,183 --> 00:01:38,658
Do they need to be searchable in
minutes, or in hours?

25
00:01:39,283 --> 00:01:41,633
And fifth, access control.

26
00:01:41,933 --> 00:01:47,208
Whether permissions are per user or per tenant
decides where filtering happens.

27
00:01:47,508 --> 00:01:51,708
Getting this wrong is the most common
way RAG systems leak data.

28
00:01:52,717 --> 00:01:54,742
The system has two halves.

29
00:01:55,042 --> 00:02:00,092
Offline, an ingestion pipeline turns raw
documents into two indexes.

30
00:02:00,392 --> 00:02:05,942
Online, the query path searches both, reranks
the results, and generates an answer.

31
00:02:06,550 --> 00:02:09,575
Let's start offline, one stage at a time.

32
00:02:10,183 --> 00:02:14,908
Parsing comes first, and it's the hidden
ceiling on everything downstream.

33
00:02:15,208 --> 00:02:20,933
Use layout-aware parsing, run OCR on
scans, and keep tables as tables.

34
00:02:21,233 --> 00:02:26,308
Then read about 200 parsed documents
yourself, before tuning anything else.

35
00:02:26,917 --> 00:02:33,192
Next, chunk along the document's structure, at
roughly 300-800 tokens per chunk.

36
00:02:33,492 --> 00:02:38,339
Store a pointer to each chunk's parent section,
so you can retrieve something small,

37
00:02:38,339 --> 00:02:41,917
and hand the model something bigger
when it needs the context.

38
00:02:42,917 --> 00:02:44,817
A quick sizing check.

39
00:02:45,117 --> 00:02:52,592
25B tokens over ten million documents is
about 2,500 tokens per document.

40
00:02:53,217 --> 00:02:58,642
At 300-800 tokens per chunk, that's
30 to 80 million chunks.

41
00:02:59,250 --> 00:03:05,025
Plan capacity for 100 million, to leave room
for overlap, context headers and

42
00:03:05,025 --> 00:03:05,550
growth.

43
00:03:06,550 --> 00:03:11,692
Then enrich each chunk with what retrieval
will need: its title, section, date,

44
00:03:11,692 --> 00:03:13,775
tenant, and access-control list.

45
00:03:14,383 --> 00:03:19,614
Optionally, prepend a short generated
sentence that places each chunk in its

46
00:03:19,614 --> 00:03:20,233
document.

47
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:23,583
Anthropic calls this contextual retrieval.

48
00:03:23,883 --> 00:03:31,202
Combined with keyword search and reranking, it
cut their top-20 retrieval failures

49
00:03:31,202 --> 00:03:32,808
from 5.7% to 1.9%.

50
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:36,392
Each chunk then goes into two indexes.

51
00:03:36,692 --> 00:03:43,014
A vector index captures meaning, and a BM25
keyword index catches exact terms, like

52
00:03:43,014 --> 00:03:44,842
product codes and names.

53
00:03:45,850 --> 00:03:48,850
Now the number everyone
forgets to compute.

54
00:03:49,150 --> 00:03:51,600
How big is the vector index?

55
00:03:52,217 --> 00:04:00,092
A hundred million vectors at 1024 dimensions,
in 32-bit floats, is about 410 GB.

56
00:04:00,392 --> 00:04:02,217
That's a lot of RAM.

57
00:04:02,817 --> 00:04:09,133
Truncate to 512 dimensions with a
Matryoshka-trained model, store each value as an

58
00:04:09,133 --> 00:04:12,292
8-bit integer, and you're at about 51 GB.

59
00:04:12,917 --> 00:04:18,567
Go further with binary or
product-quantized codes, and it's 6-13 GB.

60
00:04:18,867 --> 00:04:24,647
Those trade recall for size, so rescore the
top candidates with higher-precision

61
00:04:24,647 --> 00:04:26,092
vectors kept on SSD.

62
00:04:27,117 --> 00:04:33,538
For the index itself, HNSW graphs in memory give
the best recall and latency, if you

63
00:04:33,538 --> 00:04:35,067
can pay for the RAM.

64
00:04:35,683 --> 00:04:42,748
IVF-PQ or DiskANN live on SSD, and cost far
less per vector, at the price of more

65
00:04:42,748 --> 00:04:43,358
tuning.

66
00:04:43,983 --> 00:04:48,956
Whichever you choose, keep metadata and
permission filters in the same engine, so

67
00:04:48,956 --> 00:04:52,333
that filtering happens during the
search, not after it.

68
00:04:52,950 --> 00:04:57,875
Stream updates in as documents change, and
apply deletions immediately.

69
00:04:58,175 --> 00:05:02,150
Deletes are a compliance issue, not
just a relevance one.

70
00:05:02,750 --> 00:05:06,450
And tag every vector with the
model that produced it.

71
00:05:06,750 --> 00:05:10,900
When you change embedding models, you'll
re-embed the whole corpus.

72
00:05:11,917 --> 00:05:13,892
Now the online side.

73
00:05:14,192 --> 00:05:17,492
When a question arrives, here's
where the time goes.

74
00:05:18,117 --> 00:05:23,894
An optional rewrite step, using a small model or
rules, splits multi-part questions

75
00:05:23,894 --> 00:05:26,192
and pulls out filters like dates.

76
00:05:26,492 --> 00:05:29,942
Budget 100-300 ms.

77
00:05:30,550 --> 00:05:35,959
Hybrid retrieval runs the vector search and the
keyword search in parallel, about

78
00:05:35,959 --> 00:05:40,100
100 results each, and merges them
with reciprocal rank fusion.

79
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,900
That's 30-80 ms.

80
00:05:43,517 --> 00:05:48,886
A cross-encoder then reads each candidate
together with the question, and keeps the

81
00:05:48,886 --> 00:05:49,792
best 10 to 20.

82
00:05:50,092 --> 00:05:56,192
It's far more precise than the first
stage, for 80-200 ms on a GPU.

83
00:05:56,817 --> 00:06:02,836
Context assembly removes duplicates, expands
chunks to their parent sections, and

84
00:06:02,836 --> 00:06:04,917
orders them, in under 20 ms.

85
00:06:05,517 --> 00:06:11,992
And generation streams the answer, with the
first token after 400-800 ms.

86
00:06:12,617 --> 00:06:21,039
Add up the worst cases, and the first token
arrives in 1,400 ms, just inside the 1.5

87
00:06:21,039 --> 00:06:21,942
s target.

88
00:06:22,242 --> 00:06:27,692
In production, measure the end-to-end
distribution, rather than summing the table.

89
00:06:28,717 --> 00:06:34,044
For generation, tell the model to answer only
from the retrieved passages, to cite

90
00:06:34,044 --> 00:06:37,292
them, and to say so when the
evidence isn't there.

91
00:06:37,917 --> 00:06:42,292
Put the strongest passages at the start
and the end of the context.

92
00:06:42,592 --> 00:06:47,992
Models pay less attention to the middle of long
inputs, an effect known as lost in

93
00:06:47,992 --> 00:06:48,717
the middle.

94
00:06:49,317 --> 00:06:54,812
And cache what repeats: the system prompt, query
embeddings, and answers to frequent

95
00:06:54,812 --> 00:06:55,467
questions.

96
00:06:56,483 --> 00:06:58,133
Now the cost.

97
00:06:58,433 --> 00:07:04,308
50 queries per second, around the clock, is
about 4.3 million queries a day.

98
00:07:04,917 --> 00:07:11,292
At about 12,000 input tokens each, that's
roughly 52B input tokens a day.

99
00:07:11,592 --> 00:07:15,792
Since 50 per second is a peak, treat
that as an upper bound.

100
00:07:16,417 --> 00:07:23,041
At an illustrative one dollar per million input
tokens, that's about $52,000 a day,

101
00:07:23,041 --> 00:07:24,717
before output tokens.

102
00:07:25,317 --> 00:07:31,056
Retrieval infrastructure for 100 million
vectors costs a few thousand dollars a

103
00:07:31,056 --> 00:07:31,492
month.

104
00:07:31,792 --> 00:07:36,042
So generation dominates the bill, by
far more than ten times.

105
00:07:37,050 --> 00:07:42,650
That's why the reranker pays for itself: fewer,
better chunks means fewer tokens.

106
00:07:42,950 --> 00:07:48,223
Add routing of easy questions to smaller
models, plus caching, and those are the

107
00:07:48,223 --> 00:07:49,475
levers that matter.

108
00:07:50,483 --> 00:07:55,479
None of this matters unless you can measure it,
and the key is to measure each stage

109
00:07:55,479 --> 00:07:56,133
separately.

110
00:07:56,750 --> 00:08:03,450
Build a golden set of 500 to 2,000 real queries,
labeled by subject-matter experts.

111
00:08:03,750 --> 00:08:10,670
Target recall at 50 of at least 0.95 before
the reranker, and recall at 10 of at

112
00:08:10,670 --> 00:08:12,400
least 0.85 after it.

113
00:08:13,017 --> 00:08:18,997
Track the index's own recall against exact
search, too, so index tuning doesn't hide

114
00:08:18,997 --> 00:08:20,492
inside model changes.

115
00:08:21,117 --> 00:08:26,170
For answers, measure faithfulness to the
cited passages, correctness, citation

116
00:08:26,170 --> 00:08:30,317
precision, and whether the system
abstains when it can't answer.

117
00:08:30,617 --> 00:08:36,467
If you use an LLM as a judge, calibrate it
against about 200 human labels.

118
00:08:37,083 --> 00:08:42,158
Online, watch feedback, reformulated
queries, and clicks on citations.

119
00:08:42,458 --> 00:08:47,660
And make every change to the chunker,
embedder, index or prompt run the offline

120
00:08:47,660 --> 00:08:49,108
suite before it ships.

121
00:08:50,117 --> 00:08:53,192
Here's how the main index options compare.

122
00:08:53,492 --> 00:09:00,080
HNSW in memory gives the best recall and
latency, but it's RAM-heavy and weak under

123
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:01,192
heavy deletes.

124
00:09:01,817 --> 00:09:08,232
IVF-PQ or DiskANN on SSD are five to twenty
times cheaper in memory and scale to

125
00:09:08,232 --> 00:09:12,242
billions, but they need
rescoring and more tuning.

126
00:09:12,850 --> 00:09:18,216
A managed vector database is the fastest to
ship, but it gets expensive at 100

127
00:09:18,216 --> 00:09:22,550
million vectors, and gives you less
control over hybrid search.

128
00:09:23,150 --> 00:09:29,059
And a search engine with vector support, like
OpenSearch, Elasticsearch or Vespa,

129
00:09:29,059 --> 00:09:34,823
gives you hybrid search, filters and BM25 in
one system, at the cost of heavier

130
00:09:34,823 --> 00:09:35,625
operations.

131
00:09:36,650 --> 00:09:39,500
One tradeoff deserves its own warning.

132
00:09:39,800 --> 00:09:44,844
If you fetch the top results first and filter
permissions afterwards, a selective

133
00:09:44,844 --> 00:09:49,825
permission can leave you with zero results,
and the timing can leak information.

134
00:09:50,125 --> 00:09:53,200
Filter during the search, or
shard per tenant.

135
00:09:54,217 --> 00:09:57,617
Let's finish with the mistakes
that sink these projects.

136
00:09:57,917 --> 00:10:04,048
First: jumping straight to a vector database
and an LLM, with no targets, no eval

137
00:10:04,048 --> 00:10:06,017
set, and no capacity math.

138
00:10:06,617 --> 00:10:09,617
Second: ignoring permissions
and deletions.

139
00:10:09,917 --> 00:10:15,279
Leaked documents and stale deleted content
are the incidents that actually get

140
00:10:15,279 --> 00:10:15,967
escalated.

141
00:10:16,583 --> 00:10:19,883
Third: reporting one
end-to-end quality score.

142
00:10:20,183 --> 00:10:25,101
If you don't separate retrieval recall from
answer faithfulness, you can't tell

143
00:10:25,101 --> 00:10:26,283
which stage to fix.

144
00:10:26,883 --> 00:10:29,558
And fourth: unbounded context.

145
00:10:29,858 --> 00:10:35,808
Sending the top 50 chunks costs more, runs
slower, and actually makes answers worse.

146
00:10:36,817 --> 00:10:38,492
So, to recap.

147
00:10:38,792 --> 00:10:41,842
Treat RAG as a retrieval problem first.

148
00:10:42,142 --> 00:10:44,367
Measure each stage on its own.

149
00:10:44,667 --> 00:10:47,842
And do the sizing math before
you build anything.

150
00:10:48,850 --> 00:10:53,450
The full article, with every number and
every source, is on vidyal.ai.

151
00:10:54,050 --> 00:10:58,900
Next in the series: how to debug a RAG
system that gives wrong answers.

152
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:00,750
See you there.
